Looks good. Two small polish items:

1. `if (scope === "marked") { if (scoped === null || scoped === undefined) ... }` — the `scoped === undefined` check is unreachable given scope==="marked", but TS needs it; acceptable. Could restructure: `const scoped = scope === "marked" ? releaseNotes() : null;` then `scoped ?? ...` — but null is meaningful. Fine as is.

2. The body line: "of the releases newer than v0.1.0, `v0.1.5` carries `atomaton:adopt`. That mark is the reason this notice exists, and taking the latest release takes it too." Good.

Now, one gap in the acceptance criteria from the issue: "「すべて」の設定では従来どおり両方で立つ" — there's an existing test for unmarked with unset. My added test covers explicit `all` with unmarked. Does any test cover `all` with marked release present? Minor; the `all` path ignores marks entirely (markedReleases: null → no added line). The user's verification asks both cases under `all` — the unset path test covers "unmarked → notice", and add? Let me not over-add; but acceptance explicitly says both. Cheap to add one assertion: `all` with a marked window still writes a notice without the mark line. Actually that's the same code path (marks aren't consulted). I could rename/extend the `all` test to run both. Let me modify the `all` test to also run the marked fixture:

```ts
  test("`all` notices both windows, and says nothing about marks", () => {
    for (const notes of [unmarked, oneMarked]) {
      const r = announce(behindWith(ALL), reads("v0.2.0", { notes }));
      expect(r.status).toBe(0);
      expect(notices(r.ghCalls)).toHaveLength(1);
      expect(writeBody(...)).not.toContain(ADOPT_MARKER);
    }
  });
```
